// TC: O(n) 
// It only loops through the string once 

// SC: O(1)

// Another way to solve this:
class Solution {
public:
    int romanToInt(string s) {
        //Create an array where the index in the ASCII value of the character 
        //This acts as a blazing fast loopkup table 
        int roman[123] = {0};

        roman['I'] = 1;
        roman['V'] = 5;
        roman['X'] = 10;
        roman['L'] = 50;
        roman['C'] = 100;
        roman['D'] = 500;
        roman['M'] = 1000;

        int total = 0;

        //Loop from left to right
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            //If the current numeral is smaller than the NEXT one subtract it 
            if (i + 1 < s.length() && roman[s[i]] < roman[s[i+1]]) {
                total -= roman[s[i]];
            }
            //Otherwise add it 
            else{
                total += roman[s[i]];
            }
        }

        return total;
    }
};

TC: O(n) 
it uses single for loop 
because the valid Roman numerals never exceed 3999 (which is 15 characters long), you can technically argue that the TC is O(1) coz the loop will run for max of 15 times.

SC: O(1)